tokio-yamux 0.3.20, modified by the Puddle project
==================================================

Original: https://github.com/nervosnetwork/tentacle (directory `yamux`), published on crates.io as
`tokio-yamux`. Copyright (c) 2018 Nervos Foundation, MIT licence (the `LICENSE` file here).

Changed by Puddle, two changes:

1. `src/stream.rs`. A stream whose peer has closed its sending half (a FIN) still handles the peer's
   window updates, so the half-closed connection can move more than one window of data in the other
   direction. A writer blocked on a full window is woken by the peer's window update even when
   nobody reads the stream: the stream's frame channel holds one waker that wakes both its reader
   and its writer (0.3.20 woke the writer only through the reader).
2. `src/session.rs`. 0.3.20 stops reading the session's socket while any frame is queued for a stream
   whose reader is behind (its frame channel holds 8), so one slow reader stalls every other stream of
   the session until it catches up. Now the session keeps reading while the queued frames weigh less than
   four windows (`PENDING_WINDOWS`), counts the data each stream holds, and resets a stream that holds
   more data than its own window (a peer that ignores the window), instead of stopping; the reader of
   that stream gets a connection-reset error. Beyond four windows
   queued in all, it stops reading as before. The memory a stream that does not read can hold is its
   window, which the peer was already allowed to send.

The diff is `diff -u` of `src/stream.rs` and `src/session.rs` against the 0.3.20 release (the commits
that vendored the crate show the first change, the unchanged copy, then the fix).
`Cargo.toml` is the upstream repository's manifest with dev-dependencies, benches and examples
dropped, and the files carry an SPDX header line; nothing else differs.

This copy is temporary. Remove it, and the `tokio-yamux` entry under `[patch.crates-io]` in the
workspace `Cargo.toml`, when either of these is true:

1. A tokio-yamux release newer than 0.3.20 handles window updates after the peer's FIN. Check: in
   that release's `src/stream.rs`, `recv_frames` and `try_recv_frames` no longer return early for
   `StreamState::RemoteClosing`, and `poll_write` wakes on a window update whether or not the stream
   is read. The tests named in `README.md` for the first fix pass against the registry crate (delete
   the patch entry, run them).
2. A fork pinned by `rev` replaces it (decided by the project owner).

Until then, every tokio-yamux version bump re-applies both changes to the new release.

Condition 1 above covers the first change only. For the second, the release must also keep reading a
session whose slow streams hold less than their windows (check: the three tests named in `README.md`
under the second fix pass against the registry crate).
